  "seo": {
    "title": "Waking Up at 3am: Causes and How to Fall Back Asleep",
    "description": "Learn why waking up at 3am is a common feature of sleep biology, what triggers nighttime awakenings, and when it is worth speaking to a doctor."
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "question": "Is it normal to wake up at 3am every night?",
      "answer": "Yes, brief awakenings between sleep cycles are a normal part of human sleep architecture. It becomes a clinical concern only when you stay awake for long stretches or experience persistent daytime fatigue."
    },
    {
      "question": "Should I stay in bed if I cannot sleep?",
      "answer": "If you have been awake for around twenty minutes and feel restless, it is helpful to get out of bed. Lying in bed awake can cause your brain to associate the bed with frustration and alertness."
    },
    {
      "question": "Why do I wake up feeling wide awake at 3am?",
      "answer": "By 3am, you have cleared most of your deep sleep and accumulated sleep pressure. Combined with natural early morning temperature and hormone shifts, your brain is in a lighter sleep phase that is easier to arouse fully."
    }
  ]
